{# ---------- Works with ---------- #}
<section class="section works" id="works">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Works with</p>
    <h2>Two kinds of devices, switched two ways</h2>
    <ul class="works__list">
      <li class="works__item">
        <h3>Apple Magic Trackpad, Keyboard &amp; Mouse</h3>
        <p>
          They only pair with one computer at a time. AutoSwitch removes the pairing on the computer
          you leave and pairs again on the one you arrive at, in a few seconds.
        </p>
        <p class="works__how">Hands off by pairing<br>Mac ✓ · Windows ✓</p>
      </li>
      <li class="works__item">
        <h3>Logitech Easy-Switch keyboards &amp; mice</h3>
        <p>
          The K380 and other Easy-Switch devices keep a channel per computer. AutoSwitch tells the
          device to hop to the other computer's channel, and finds that channel by itself.
        </p>
        <p class="works__how">Switches channel on request<br>Mac ✓ · Windows ✓</p>
      </li>
      <li class="works__item works__item--other">
        <h3>Everything else</h3>
        <p>
          ZMK keyboards, game controllers, headphones: AutoSwitch tells you what's possible for each
          one, shows its status, and points you to the easy fixes (like a USB adapter on the KVM).
        </p>
        <p class="works__how"><a href="/devices/">See the device guide →</a></p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

{# ---------- The problem ---------- #}
<section class="section section--tint" id="problem">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
    <h2>The Bluetooth shuffle</h2>
    <p class="section__lead">
      A KVM switch shares your monitor, keyboard and mouse between two computers. Plugged-in things
      switch along. Bluetooth things <em>don't</em>. Some only pair with one computer at a time (hello,
      Magic Trackpad); others want you to press a channel key on every single device. So every
      switch goes something like this:
    </p>
    <ol class="shuffle">
      <li>
        <span class="shuffle__num">1</span>
        <h3>Open Bluetooth settings</h3>
        <p>On the computer you're leaving. Find your trackpad, keyboard or mouse in the list.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="shuffle__num">2</span>
        <h3>Forget the device</h3>
        <p>"Are you sure?" Yes. It's fine. It's not personal.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="shuffle__num">3</span>
        <h3>Pair it on the other one</h3>
        <p>Search. Wait. Click it. Wait some more. Hold your breath.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="shuffle__num">4</span>
        <h3>Repeat after lunch</h3>
        <p>And again before your 3 pm call. Forever.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="punchline">AutoSwitch KVM does the shuffle for you. Every single time. In a few seconds.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ---------- How it works ---------- #}
<section class="section" id="how">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
    <h2>Set it up once. Then forget it exists.</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <span class="steps__badge">1</span>
        <h3>Install it on both computers</h3>
        <p>The one being left lets go of your devices, so the other one can grab them. Teamwork.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="steps__badge">2</span>
        <h3>Show it your KVM</h3>
        <p>
          Press <em>Learn</em>, switch the KVM away and back. AutoSwitch notices which USB gadget
          comes and goes with the switch, and that's how it knows which computer is in charge.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="steps__badge">3</span>
        <h3>Pick your devices</h3>
        <p>Keyboard, mouse, trackpad. AutoSwitch tells you which ones it can switch, and you choose the order.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="shot">
        <img src="/assets/img/mac-panel.png" alt="AutoSwitch KVM in the macOS menu bar: three devices, two connected, one connecting" width="300" height="319" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>On the Mac, it lives in the menu bar.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="shot">
        <img src="/assets/img/windows-panel.png" alt="AutoSwitch KVM in the Windows tray: three devices, two connected, one connecting" width="312" height="323" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>On Windows, it lives in the tray.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ---------- Features ---------- #}
<section class="section section--tint" id="features">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">The good stuff</p>
    <h2>Small app. Big sigh of relief.</h2>
    <ul class="features">
      <li>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 12h12m0 0-4-4m4 4-4 4M20 5v14"/></svg>
        <h3>Hands-off switching</h3>
        <p>Devices connect when your computer is picked and step aside when it isn't. Nothing to click.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 20s-7-4.4-7-10a4 4 0 0 1 7-2.6A4 4 0 0 1 19 10c0 5.6-7 10-7 10z"/></svg>
        <h3>Handles the loyal ones</h3>
        <p>Some devices only love one computer at a time. AutoSwitch unpairs on the way out and pairs on the way in.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M5 18h14M7 14h10M9 10h6M11 6h2"/></svg>
        <h3>Brings the whole gang</h3>
        <p>Keyboard, mouse, trackpad: several devices at once, in your order, with profiles like "Desk" and "Travel".</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 7h16M4 12h10M4 17h7"/></svg>
        <h3>Speaks Easy-Switch</h3>
        <p>Logitech keyboards and mice change channel when AutoSwitch asks. It even reads which computer is on which channel.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M20 14.5A8 8 0 0 1 9.5 4a8 8 0 1 0 10.5 10.5z"/></svg>
        <h3>Naps when you nap</h3>
        <p>Your computer goes to sleep, your devices are released. It wakes, everything catches up.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 5v14M15 5v14"/></svg>
        <h3>Pause any time</h3>
        <p>One click to pause, plus Connect all / Release all buttons and optional keyboard shortcuts.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M20 12a8 8 0 1 1-2.3-5.7M20 4v5h-5"/></svg>
        <h3>Keeps itself fresh</h3>
        <p>Updates download quietly in the background. Restart when it suits you.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 3l7 3v6c0 4.5-3 7.5-7 9-4-1.5-7-4.5-7-9V6z"/></svg>
        <h3>Minds its own business</h3>
        <p>No account, no analytics. The only thing it talks to is GitHub, to check for updates.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

{# ---------- Download ---------- #}
<section class="section" id="download">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Download</p>
    <h2>Free. For both of your computers.</h2>
    <div class="downloads">
      <article class="dl">
        <h3>macOS</h3>
        <p class="dl__req">macOS 13 Ventura or later · Apple silicon &amp; Intel</p>
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="{{ site.download.mac }}">Download for Mac</a>
        <p class="dl__note">
          Open the DMG and drag AutoSwitch KVM into Applications. The first launch gets blocked
          (we haven't bought Apple's fancy certificate yet): go to <strong>System Settings ▸
          Privacy &amp; Security</strong> and click <strong>Open Anyway</strong>. Then allow
          Bluetooth when it asks: without it, AutoSwitch can't reach your devices.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="dl">
        <h3>Windows</h3>
        <p class="dl__req">Windows 10 (1809+) or 11 · x64 · <a href="{{ site.download.windowsArm }}">ARM64 here</a></p>
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="{{ site.download.windows }}">Download for Windows</a>
        <p class="dl__note">
          Run the installer, no admin needed. If SmartScreen says "Windows protected your PC",
          click <strong>More info ▸ Run anyway</strong> (same story: no fancy certificate yet).
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="downloads__more">Every version and its release notes are on <a href="{{ site.releases }}">GitHub Releases</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ---------- FAQ ---------- #}
<section class="section section--tint" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Questions</p>
    <h2>Good to know</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details>
        <summary>Do I really need it on both computers?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. A device like the Magic Trackpad can only be paired with one computer at a time, so the
          computer you're leaving has to let go before the other one can pair. Each copy handles its
          own side of that handoff.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Will it work with my KVM switch?</summary>
        <p>
          Very likely. It works with any KVM that switches USB between the computers, which is how
          almost all of them share your keyboard and mouse. AutoSwitch watches for the USB device
          (usually a hub inside the KVM) that appears and disappears with each switch.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which Bluetooth devices work?</summary>
        <p>
          Devices that hold one pairing and accept the next computer once released, like the Apple
          Magic Trackpad, Keyboard and Mouse: those switch automatically, and so do Logitech
          Easy-Switch devices like the K380. Keyboards with their own host keys (ZMK) and
          game controllers can't be switched by any app;
          AutoSwitch tells you so when you add one, and shows their status.
          <a href="/devices/">The device guide</a> explains all four kinds.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Why does a switch take a few seconds?</summary>
        <p>
          The computer you switched to has to wait until the other one lets go of the device, and
          Bluetooth pairing itself takes a moment. AutoSwitch retries quietly until the device is
          free, so you don't have to.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does it collect any data?</summary>
        <p>
          No. There's no account and no analytics. Your settings stay on your computer. The app only
          contacts GitHub to check for updates.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is it really free?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. The source code is on <a href="{{ site.github }}">GitHub</a>. Found a bug or have
          an idea? <a href="{{ site.issues }}">Open an issue</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ---------- Final call ---------- #}
<section class="finale">
  <div class="wrap finale__inner">
    <h2>Go on. Switch something.</h2>
    <p>Your keyboard, mouse and trackpad are ready to follow you around.</p>
    <a class="btn btn--primary btn--big" href="#download">Get AutoSwitch KVM</a>
  </div>
</section>
